## Why this matters even though the option is internal

`@tanstack/solid-router@2.0.0-rc.0`'s `useRouterState` creates its select memo exactly this way (`useRouterState.js`: `Solid.createMemo((prev) => {...}, { transparent: true })`), so on the current RC line every `useRouterState` call poisons the hydration of all later siblings. We hit this in a real app as a page whose footer buttons were permanently dead after hydration. (filed there too: https://github.com/TanStack/router/issues/8096)

## Where it seems to live

From reading `solid-js/dist/solid.js` 2.0.0-rc.0: `hydratedCreateMemo` short-circuits for transparent memos — `if (!sharedConfig.hydrating || options?.transparent) return createMemo$1(compute, options)` — while non-transparent memos go through `hydrateSignalLike`. The server type docs say transparent owners must leave "hydration-id chains untouched"; the observed key shift (every sibling after the creation point expects id N but the DOM carries N+1) suggests the client-side transparent path still consumes a child id (or the SSR side skips one), so the claim cursor walks off by one at the first sibling boundary after the memo.

### Describe the bug

#3093 bounded the error header because a value past a receiver's limit makes the whole response unreadable. Two more response headers are composed from author-derived content at full length, with no equivalent bound — and both of them break a response that carries a **successful** mutation.

```js
// packages/web/server-functions/src/server.ts:1379-1385
function maskRedirect(headers, response, requestUrl) {
  const target = response.headers && response.headers.get("Location");
  if (target) {
    headers.set(REDIRECT_HEADER, `${response.status} ${new URL(target, requestUrl)}`);
  }
  headers.delete("Location");
}
```

```js
// packages/web/src/response.ts:150
  revalidate !== undefined && headers.set(REVALIDATE_HEADER, revalidate.toString());
```

Measured on `next` (`d6a4a52f`): a redirect to a target with a 20,000-character query produces a 20,036-character `X-Server-Function-Redirect`; a `respond(..., { revalidate: [...2000 keys] })` produces a 62,889-character `X-Revalidate`. Over a real socket both stop being responses:

```
in process  go     x-server-function-redirect: 20036 characters
in process  reval  x-revalidate: 62889 characters
fetch       go     FETCH FAILED: Headers Overflow Error
fetch       reval  FETCH FAILED: Headers Overflow Error
8 KiB       go     HPE_HEADER_OVERFLOW: Parse Error: Header overflow
8 KiB       reval  HPE_HEADER_OVERFLOW: Parse Error: Header overflow
```

The write committed. The caller gets a network error.

**Where the ceiling actually is.** Bisected against the same handler:

| receiver | redirect target | revalidate keys (`orders:tenant-42:list:page-N`, 28 chars) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| undici, `fetch`'s 16 KiB default | fails at ~16,100 chars of query | fails at **526 keys** |
| an 8 KiB budget (nginx's `proxy_buffer_size` is one page for the WHOLE upstream header block) | fails at ~7,900 chars | fails at **261 keys** |
| Chrome 152 | passes a 131,107-char header; fails at ~262 KB | — |

So the browser is not the binding constraint; the proxy in front of the app is, and undici is — which is every Node-side caller, SSR-time included.

That table also separates the two headers honestly:

- **`X-Revalidate` reaches the ceiling in ordinary use.** 261 invalidated cache keys behind a default nginx is not a pathological mutation; a bulk edit or a namespaced cache gets there. This is the half I would call a live defect.
- **The redirect target does not, on its own.** A legitimate destination past 8 KB is unusual — nginx's own request-line buffer is 8 KB, so such a URL could not be requested back — and an OAuth `state` or a search query is normally tens to hundreds of bytes. I did not measure real-world targets and will not claim a number. What reaches the ceiling is a target built from *unbounded input*: an echoed `?returnTo=`, a search string put straight into the redirect, a nested callback chain. That is an app bug today; the runtime's answer to it is a socket error rather than anything the author can see.

